.SH "DESCRIPTION"
.sp
\fBsaptune\fP is designed to automate the configuration recommendations from
SAP and SUSE to run an SAP application on SLES for SAP. These
configuration recommendations are normally referred to as SAP Notes. So
some dedicated SAP Notes are the base for the work of \fBsaptune\fP.
Additional some best practice guides are added as Note definitions to
optimize the system for some really special cases.
.sp
\fBsaptune\fP can apply settings for a single Note definition or can set all
settings for a predefined solution, which is a collection of several SAP
Notes. Additionally it can revert all these settings back to the
previous system settings.
.sp
It can verify, if the current system configuration is in sync with a
specific Note definition or a defined solution and displays the
differences.
\" _strm_3.2.0_start
.sp
Additionally, it can simulate the setting of a single SAP
Note definition or a defined solution and will display the changes which
will be done to the system.
\" _strm_3.2.0_end
.sp
\fBsaptune\fP does not only set kernel values (like \fBsysctl\fP does), but also
values like cpu governor, energy perf bias, force latency (dma latency)
and the disk I/O scheduler. Additionally it will check/verify, if
suitable rpm versions are installed and special kernel command line
values are set, according to the relevant SAP Notes. So \fBsaptune\fP checks
and applies values in various locations during runtime like
.sp
\fI/proc/sys/\fP, \fI/proc/sys/vm/\fP, \fI/proc/sys/kernel\fP, \fI/proc/sys/fs\fP,
\fI/sys/block/*/queue/scheduler\fP, \fI/sys/block/*/queue/nr_requests\fP,
\fI/sys/devices/system/cpu/*/cpufreq/scaling_governor\fP,
\fI/sys/devices/system/cpu/\(rs*/cpuidle/state*/latency\fP,
\fI/sys/devices/system/cpu/*/cpuidle/state*/disable\fP, \fI/dev/shm\fP,
\fI/etc/security/limits.d/\fP, \fI/etc/systemd/logind.conf.d/\fP and some others.
.sp
To do most of these settings \fBsaptune\fP needs to run with root privilege.
So please be in mind that users who run \fBsaptune\fP are considered
privileged users with administrative rights.
.sp
By default \fBsaptune\fP is printing all results to stdout, Errors and
Warnings are printed to stderr. By using \fB\-\-format FORMAT\fP this
behavior can be changed.
.br
Supported formats are:
.sp
\fBjson\fP
.RS 4
Print all results in a machine readable json output format defined by
the schemata delivered in _/usr/share/saptune/schemas/.
.RE
.sp
\fBsaptune\fP does no longer use \fBtuned\fP(8) to restart after a system reboot.
It is using its own systemd service named \fBsaptune.service\fP.
.br
Starting with version 3.2 and operating system version 15SP4 we ship
the \fBsaptune.service\fP with enhanced security\-related features like
systemd hardening. But please keep in mind that all security measures
only apply, if \fBsaptune\fP is invoked by its systemd unit!
.sp
We decided to have only ONE solution applied, but multiple Notes. Each
Note is applied exactly once.
\" _strm_3.2.0_start
.SH "DAEMON ACTIONS \- ATTENTION: DEPRECATED"
.sp
\fBstart\fP
.RS 4
As \fBsaptune\fP no longer uses \fBtuned\fP, this action is internally linked to
\fBsaptune service takeover\fP. See description below.
.RE
.sp
\fBstatus\fP
.RS 4
As \fBsaptune\fP no longer uses \fBtuned\fP, this action is internally linked to
\fBsaptune service status\fP. See description below.
.RE
.sp
\fBstop\fP
.RS 4
As \fBsaptune\fP no longer uses \fBtuned\fP, this action is internally linked to
\fBsaptune service disablestop\fP. See description below.
.RE
\" _strm_3.2.0_end
.SH "SERVICE ACTIONS"
.sp
\fBstart\fP
.RS 4
Start \fBsaptune\fP service and apply a set of optimizations to the system,
if solutions or Notes were selected during a previous call of \fBsaptune\fP.
If the service is enabled, the tuning will be automatically activated
upon system boot.
.br
It redirects to \fBsystemctl start saptune.service\fP
.br
Success is reported on stdout, errors including systemd error messages
are printed on stderr. The action gets logged.
+
It will fail in case \fBsapconf.service\fP is running or has exited or save
state files are present.
+
If the action was successfully the exit code is 0, otherwise 1.
.RE
.sp
\fBstatus\fP
.RS 4
Reports the following status information on stdout:
