.RE
.sp
This information is not logged, but only printed to stdout.
.sp
If saptune.service is \fBnot\fP \*(Aqactive\*(Aq the exit code is 1. If the system
is \*(Aq\fBnot tuned\fP\*(Aq \- which means no Note or Solution is enabled \- the
exit code is 3. If the system is tuned, but the tuning is \fBnot
compliant\fP the exit code is 4. Otherwise the exit code is 0.
.sp
\fBstop\fP
.RS 4
Stop \fBsaptune\fP service and revert all optimizations that were previously
applied by \fBsaptune\fP. If the service is disabled, the tuning will no
longer automatically activate upon boot.
.br
It redirects to \fBsystemctl stop saptune.service\fP
.br
Success is reported on stdout, errors including systemd error messages
are printed on stderr. The action gets logged.
.br
\~
.br
If the action was successfully the exit code is 0, otherwise 1.
.RE
.sp
\fBrestart\fP
.RS 4
Revert all optimizations that were previously applied by \fBsaptune\fP. And
after that apply a set of optimizations to the system, if Solutions or
Notes were selected during a previous call of \fBsaptune\fP.
.br
It redirects to \fBsystemctl restart saptune.service\fP
.br
Success is reported on stdout, errors including systemd error messages
are printed on stderr. The action gets logged.
.br
\~
.br
If the action was successfully the exit code is 0, otherwise 1.
.br
\~
.br
If \fIIGNORE_RELOAD\fP is set to \fByes\fP in the \fBsaptune\fP configuration
file the action \fBrestart\fP will do \fBnothing\fP. The reason will be
logged. See the \fBNOTE\fP section at the end of the man page for more
information.
.RE
.sp
\fBenable\fP
.RS 4
Enables the saptune service. To activate the tuning, the \fBsaptune\fP
service needs to be started. But as the service is now enabled, the
tuning will automatically activated upon system boot.
.br
It redirects to \fBsystemctl enable saptune.service\fP
.br
Success is reported on stdout, errors including systemd error messages
are printed on stderr. The action gets logged.
.br
\~
.br
It will fail in case \fBsapconf.service\fP is running or has exited or save
state files are present.
.br
\~
.br
If the action was successfully the exit code is 0, otherwise 1.
.RE
.sp
\fBdisable\fP
.RS 4
Disable the \fBsaptune\fP service. To revert all optimizations that were
previously applied by \fBsaptune\fP, the \fBsaptune\fP service needs to be
stopped. But as the service is now disabled, the tuning will no longer
automatically activated upon system boot.
.br
It redirects to \fBsystemctl disable saptune.service\fP
.br
Success is reported on stdout, errors including systemd error messages
are printed on stderr. The action gets logged. *
\~
.br
If the action was successfully the exit code is 0, otherwise 1.
.RE
.sp
\fBenablestart\fP
.RS 4
Enables and start the \fBsaptune\fP service and apply a set of optimizations
to the system, if Solutions or Notes were selected during a previous
call of \fBsaptune\fP. As the service is now enabled, the tuning will
automatically activated upon system boot.
.br
Calls \fBsystemctl enable saptune.service\fP and \fBsystemctl start saptune.service\fP
in this order.
.br
Success is reported on stdout, errors including systemd error messages
are printed on stderr. The action gets logged.
.br
\~
.br
If the action was successfully the exit code is 0, otherwise 1.
.RE
.sp
\fBdisablestop\fP
.RS 4
Disable and stop the \fBsaptune\fP service and revert all optimizations that
were previously applied by \fBsaptune\fP. As the service is now disabled,
the tuning will no longer automatically activated upon system boot.
.br
Calls \fBsystemctl disable saptune.service\fP and \fBsystemctl stop saptune.service\fP
in this order.
.br
Success is reported on stdout, errors including systemd error messages
are printed on stderr. The action gets logged.
.br
\~
.br
If the action was successfully the exit code is 0, otherwise 1.
.RE
.sp
\fBtakeover\fP
.RS 4
This action is meant to start and enable \fBsaptune\fP service where
competing services like sapconf and/or tuned get stopped and disabled.
This is the modern replacement for the obsolete \fBsaptune daemon start\fP
.br
Calls \fBsystemctl enablestart saptune.service\fP after stopping and
disabling \fBsapconf.service\fP and \fBtuned.service\fP.
.br
Success is reported on stdout, errors including systemd error messages
are printed on stderr. The action gets logged.
.br
\~
.br
If the action was successfully the exit code is 0, otherwise 1.
.RE
.sp
\fBATTENTION:\fP
.RS 4
\fBsaptune\fP is able to start/stop/enable/disable systemd units, but on
boot the outcome depends on the order of execution.
.br
\~
.br
If \fBsaptune\fP is starting (or stopping) a systemd service (\fB[service]\fP
section) it might happen, that the action gets reverted later by
systemd because that service is disabled (or enabled) and executed
after \fBsaptune.service\fP.
.br
\~
.br
Similar a service enabled (or disabled) by \fBsaptune\fP might already be
stopped (or started) by systemd, because it came before
\fBsaptune.service\fP.
.br
\~
.br
If the execution order cannot be assured by service dependencies, it
is recommended to set both (\*(Aqstart,enable\*(Aq or \*(Aqstop,disable\*(Aq) in a
Note definition or an Override.
.RE
.SH "NOTE ACTIONS"
.sp
Note denotes either a SAP Note, a vendor specific tuning definition or
SUSE recommendation article.
.sp
\fBapply\fP
.RS 4
Apply optimization settings specified in the Note. The Note will be
automatically activated upon system boot if the \fBsaptune\fP service is
enabled.
.br
\~
.br
If a Note definition contains a \fB[reminder]\fP section, this section
will be printed after the Note has applied successfully. It will be
highlighted with red color.
.br
\~
.br
A Note can only be applied once.
.br
\~
.br
ATTENTION: Please be in mind: If a Note definition to be applied
contains parameter settings which are likewise set before by an
already applied Note these settings get be overwritten.
.br
The last comes, the last wins, it\(cqs all about \*(Aqorder\*(Aq.
.br
\~
.br
So be careful when applying Solutions or Notes or when reverting
Notes, especially if these Notes are part of an already applied
Solution. You can re\-apply such a Note, but the order \- and may be the
resulting parameter settings \- will be unlike before.
.br
Special attention is needed, if customer or vendor specific Notes from
\fI/etc/saptune/extra\fP are used.
.br
\~
.br
But \fBsaptune\fP will detect such conflicting settings during \fBsaptune note verify\fP
or \fBsaptune verify applied\fP and will print a footnote with a
pointer to the \*(Aqwinning\*(Aq Note.
.br
This conflict detection will not work if the verify command is used on
non\-applied Notes.
.RE
.sp
\fBlist\fP
.RS 4
List all SAP Notes, vendor specific tuning definitions and SUSE
recommendation articles that \fBsaptune\fP is capable of implementing.
.br
\~
.br
Currently implemented Notes are marked with \*(Aq\fB+\fP\*(Aq, if manually
enabled, \*(Aq\fB*\fP\*(Aq, if enabled by Solutions or \*(Aq\fB\-\fP\*(Aq, if a Note belonging
to an enabled Solution was reverted manually. In all cases the Notes
are highlighted with green color.
.br
If an \fBoverride\fP file exists for a NoteID, the Note is marked with
\*(Aq\fBO\fP\*(Aq and a customer or vendor specific Note is marked with \*(Aq\fBC\fP\*(Aq.
.br
If we detect a \fBconflict\fP with Notes the related entry in the list is
marked with a leading \*(Aq\fB!\fP\*(Aq and the line is highlighted with yellow
color. Look at the log file for details like warning messages
addressing the problem.
.br
Currently we report a conflict if a customer or vendor specific Note
has the \fBsame\fP name as a shipped Note. In this case the shipped Note
will take precedence over the custom Note.
.RE
.sp
\fBenabled\fP
.RS 4
Print all currently enabled Notes as a list separated by blanks
without trailing line feed.
.RE
.sp
\fBapplied\fP
.RS 4
Print all currently applied Notes as a list separated by blanks
without trailing line feed.
.RE
.sp
\fBverify\fP
.RS 4
If a Note ID is specified, \fBsaptune\fP verifies the currently running
system against the recommendations specified in this Note.
.br
Without a Note all enabled Notes and all (even reverted) Notes of
an enabled Solution are verified.
If the keyword \fBapplied\fP is given instead of a Note, only all applied
Notes are verified.
.br
\~
.br
The output is table with the columns:
.br
\~
.br
\fBSAPNote, Version\fP shows the Note name and the
.br
\fBParameter\fP the system parameter that gets tuned
.br
\fBExpected\fP shows the values read from the Note definition file
.br
\fBOverride\fP shows the values found in an \fBoverride\fP file
.br
\fBActual\fP shows the current system value
.br
\fBCompliant\fP shows \fByes\fP, if the \*(AqExpected\*(Aq and \*(AqActual\*(Aq value matches,
or \fBno\fP, if there is no match.
.br
\~
.br
In some rows you can find references to footnotes containing
additional information. They may explain, why a value does not match.
.br
\~
.br
If a Note definition contains a \fB[reminder]\fP section, this section
will be printed below the table and the footnotes. It will be
highlighted with red color.
.br
\~
.br
By using the command line argument \fB\-\-show\-non\-compliant\fP it is
possible to limit the verify output to show only non\-compliant
parameter. The output will \fBnot\fP be colorized even that a \fBcolor
scheme\fP is defined.
.br
\~
.br
It is possible to use a \fBcolor scheme\fP for the verify output table.
.br
The \fBcolor scheme\fP can be given as a command line argument
\fB\-\-colorscheme SCHEME\fP or as parameter \- value pair \fBCOLOR_SCHEME
SCHEME\fP in the saptune command \fBsaptune configure COLOR_SCHEME\fP.
.br
To reset the \fBcolor scheme\fP use the command \fBsaptune configure COLOR_SCHEME\fP.
.br
Possible \fBcolor schemes\fP are:
.br
\~
.br
\fBfull\-green\-zebra\fP \- whole line is colored green (compliant) or red (not compliant)
.br
\fBfull\-blue\-zebra\fP \- whole line is colored blue (compliant) or yellow (not compliant)
.br
\fBcmpl\-green\-zebra\fP \- only the content in the Compliant column is
colored green (compliant) or red (not compliant)
.br
\fBcmpl\-blue\-zebra\fP \- only the content in the Compliant column is
colored blue (compliant) or yellow (not compliant)
.br
\fBfull\-red\-noncmpl\fP \- only the whole line of the not compliant
parameter is colored red
.br
\fBfull\-yellow\-noncmpl\fP \- only the whole line of the not compliant
parameter is colored yellow
.br
\fBred\-noncmpl\fP \- only the content in the Compliant column of the not
compliant parameter is colored red
.br
\fByellow\-noncmpl\fP \- only the content in the Compliant column of the not
compliant parameter is colored yellow
.br
\~
.br
The default, if no \fBcolor scheme\fP is given, is \fBfull\-red\-noncmpl\fP. If
an unknown \fBcolor scheme\fP is given in the command line or in the
config file, non\-colorized, simple black text is printed.
.br
\~
.br
The \*(Aqfinal lines\*(Aq with the overall result of the verify operation are
colored green (compliant) or red (not compliant) independent from the
chosen \fBcolor scheme\fP
.RE
\" _strm_3.2.0_start
.sp
\fBsimulate \- ATTENTION: deprecated\fP
.RS 4
Show all changes that will be applied to the system if the specified
Note is applied. As a result you will see a table containing the
following columns:
.br
\~
.br
\fBSAPNote, Version\fP shows the Note name and the
.br
\fBParameter\fP the system parameter that gets tuned
.br
\fBValue set\fP shows the current system value
.br
\fBValue expected\fP shows the values read from the Note definition file
.br
\fBOverride\fP shows the values found in an \fBoverride\fP file
.br
\fBComment\fP shows references to \fBfootnotes\fP containing additional
information. They may explain, why a value will not be set by \fBsaptune\fP.
.br
\~
.br
If a Note definition contains a \fB[reminder]\fP section, this section
will be printed below the table and the footnotes. It will be
highlighted with red color.
.RE
\" _strm_3.2.0_end
.sp
\fBedit\fP
.RS 4
This allows to edit the values of the customer or vendor specific Note
definitions in \fB/etc/saptune/extra\fP. An editor will be launched to
allow changing the Note definitions. The editor is defined by the
\fBEDITOR\fP environment variable. If not set editor defaults to
\fI/usr/bin/vim\fP.
.br
\~
.br
You can change already available parameters and values or you can add
new parameters and values or additional sections with parameter value
pairs.
.br
\~
.br
If the Note is currently applied and/or an override file exists,
\fBsaptune\fP will remind you to take care of this situation.
.RE
.sp
\fBcustomise\fP
.RS 4
This allows to customize the values of the \fBsaptune\fP Note definitions.
The Note definition file will be copied from
\fI/usr/share/saptune/notes\fP or \fI/etc/saptune/extra\fP to the override
location at \fI/etc/saptune/override\fP, if the file does not exist
already. After that an editor will be launched to allow changing the
Note definitions. The editor is defined by the \fBEDITOR\fP environment
variable. If not set editor defaults to \fI/usr/bin/vim\fP.
.br
\~
.br
You can only change the value from already available parameters of the
Note. But you are not able to add new parameters.
.br
\~
.br
If you want to use new parameters to tune the system, please create
your own custom Note definition file in \fI/etc/saptune/extra\fP.
.br
\~
.br
You can prevent a parameter from being changed by leaving the
parameter value in the override file empty. The parameter will be
marked as \*(Aquntouched\*(Aq in the override column of the verify table.
.br
\~
.br
The values from the override files will take precedence over the
values from \fI/usr/share/saptune/notes\fP or \fI/etc/saptune/extra\fP. In
such case you will not lose your customized Notes between \fBsaptune\fP or
vendor updates.
.br
The \fBsaptune\fP options \*(Aqlist\*(Aq, \*(Aqverify\*(Aq and \*(Aqsimulate\*(Aq will mark the
existence of an override file and the contained values.
.br
\~
.br
ATTENTION: Creating or changing an override file just changes the
configuration \fIinside\fP this Note definition file, but does not change
the \fIrunning\fP configuration of the system.
.br
That means: When creating or changing an override file for an \fBalready
applied\fP Note definition, please do a \fBsaptune note revert NOTEID\fP
and then apply this Note again, to get the changes take effect.
.RE
.sp
\fBcreate\fP
.RS 4
This allows to create own Note definition files in
\fI/etc/saptune/extra\fP. The Note definition file will be created from a
template file into the location \fI/etc/saptune/extra\fP, if the file does
not exist already. After that an editor will be launched to allow
changing the Note definitions. The editor is defined by the \fBEDITOR\fP
environment variable. If not set editor defaults to \fI/usr/bin/vim\fP. You
need to choose an unique NoteID for this operation. Use \fBsaptune\fP note
list* to find the already used NoteIDs.
.RE
.sp
\fBrefresh *ATTENTION:\fP experimental*
.RS 4
Identifies and activates changed parameter settings of a Note
definition. The changes get active without first reverting the \*(Aqold\*(Aq
settings, so the tuning of the system gets not interrupted.
.br
\~
.br
ATTENTION:
.br
This action is currently in the state \fBexperimental\fP!
.br
\~
.br
Currently only parameter of section \fB[sysctl]\fP, \fB[sys]\fP and \fB[vm]\fP are
supported by the \fIrefresh\fP action.
.br
\~
.br
Parameter of section \fB[version]\fP, \fB[rpm]\fP, \fB[grub]\fP, \fB[fs]\fP and
\fB[reminder]\fP are only checked by \fBsaptune\fP, but not applied. So there is
nothing to do during refresh of a Note and these sections are skipped
without notice.
.br
\~
.br
Parameter of all other sections are currently \fBnot\fP supported by the
\fIrefresh\fP action and \fBnot\fP evaluated. If such parameter are available
in the to be refreshed Note an info is logged per affected parameter.
.RE
.sp
\fBrevert\fP
.RS 4
Revert optimization settings carried out by the Note, and the Note
will no longer be activated automatically upon system boot.
.RE
.sp
\fBrevertall\fP
.RS 4
Revert optimization settings carried out by all applied Notes, and the
Notes will no longer be activated automatically upon system boot.
.RE
.sp
\fBshow\fP
.RS 4
Print content of Note definition file to stdout
.RE
.sp
\fBdelete\fP
.RS 4
This allows to delete a customer or vendor specific Note definition
file including the corresponding override file if available. A
confirmation is needed to finish the action.
.br
\~
.br
ATTENTION:
.br
Note definition files shipped by the \fBsaptune\fP package \- so called
\fIinternal\fP \fBsaptune\fP Note definition files \- \fBmust not\fP be deleted.
There will be an appropriate error message.
.br
If a corresponding override file is available, there will be the
possibility to delete this file instead.
.br
\~
.br
ATTENTION:
.br
If the Note is already applied, the command will be terminated with
the information, that the Note first needs to be reverted before it
can be deleted.
.RE
.sp
\fBrename\fP
.RS 4
This allows to rename a customer or vendor specific Note definition
file to a new name. If a corresponding override file is available,
this file will be renamed too. A confirmation is needed to finish the
action.
.br
If the \fBnew\fP Note definition name already exists the command will be
terminated with a respective message.
.br
\~
.br
ATTENTION:
.br
Note definition files shipped by the \fBsaptune\fP package \- so called
\fIinternal\fP \fBsaptune\fP Note definition files \- and their corresponding
override files, if available, \fBmust not\fP be renamed. There will be an
appropriate error message.
.br
\~
.br
ATTENTION:
.br
If the Note is already applied, the command will be terminated with
the information, that the Note first needs to be reverted before it
can be renamed.
.RE
.SH "SOLUTION ACTIONS"
.sp
A Solution is a collection of one or more Notes. Activation of a
Solution will activate all associated Notes.
.br
The Solution definitions shipped with saptune can be found in the
directory \fI/usr/share/saptune/sols\fP or vendor/customer specific Solution
definitions can be found in the directory \fI/etc/saptune/extra\fP.
.sp
It\(cqs not possible to combine Solutions, there can only be \fBone\fP
Solution enabled. But it is possible to change a Solution definition by
using an override file in \fI/etc/saptune/override\fP or by creating a
custom specific Solution definition in the directory
\fI/etc/saptune/extra\fP.
.sp
The following Solution definitions are currently shipped with saptune:
.TS
allbox tab(:);
ltB ltB.
T{
.sp
SOLUTION
T}:T{
.sp
Definition
T}
.T&
lt lt.
T{
.sp
BOBJ
T}:T{
.sp
Solution for running SAP BusinessObjects.
T}
T{
.sp
HANA
T}:T{
.sp
Solution for running an SAP HANA database.
T}
T{
.sp
MAXDB
T}:T{
.sp
Solution for running an SAP MaxDB database.
T}
T{
.sp
NETWEAVER
T}:T{
.sp
Solution for running SAP NetWeaver application servers.
T}
T{
.sp
NETWEAVER+HANA
T}:T{
.sp
Solution for running both SAP application servers and SAP HANA on the same host.
T}
T{
.sp
NETWEAVER+MAXDB
T}:T{
.sp
Solution for running both SAP application servers and SAP MaxDB on the same host.
T}
T{
.sp
S4HANA\-APPSERVER
T}:T{
.sp
Solution for running SAP S/4HANA application servers.
T}
T{
.sp
S4HANA\-APP+DB
T}:T{
.sp
Solution for running both SAP S/4HANA application servers and SAP HANA on the same host.
T}
T{
.sp
S4HANA\-DBSERVER
T}:T{
.sp
Solution for running the SAP HANA database of an SAP S/4HANA installation.
T}
T{
.sp
SAP\-ASE
T}:T{
.sp
Solution for running an SAP Adaptive Server Enterprise database.
T}
.TE
.sp
.RS 3
.ll -.6i
.sp
Syntax of the Solution definition file names: \fI<SolutionName>.sol\fP
.br
e.g. \fIV4711.sol\fP
.br
.RE
.ll
.sp
\fBapply\fP
.RS 4
Apply optimization settings recommended by the Solution. These
settings will be automatically activated upon system boot if the
\fBsaptune\fP service is enabled.
.br
Only one Solution can be applied at the time. If a Solution is
already applied, \fBsaptune\fP will ask to replace the Solution.
With \fB\-\-force\fP the new Solution will be applied without request.
.RE
.sp
\fBlist\fP
.RS 4
List all Solution names that \fBsaptune\fP is capable of implementing.
.br
The currently implemented Solution is marked with \*(Aq\fB*\fP\*(Aq and is
highlighted with green color. A deprecated Solution is marked with
\*(Aq\fBD\fP\*(Aq.
.br
If an \fBoverride\fP file exists for a Solution, the Solution is marked
with \*(Aq\fBO\fP\*(Aq. A custom specific Solution is marked with \*(Aq\fBC\fP\*(Aq.
.br
If a Note belonging to an enabled Solution is reverted manually, the
Note is highlighted with red color (instead of green) and is crossed
out.
.br
If we detect a \fBconflict\fP with Solutions the related entry in the list
is marked with a leading \*(Aq\fB!\fP\*(Aq and the line is highlighted with yellow
color. Look at the log file for details like warning messages
addressing the problem.
.br
Currently we report a conflict if a customer or vendor specific
Solution has the \fBsame\fP name as a shipped Solution. In this case the
shipped Solution will take precedence over the custom Solution.
.RE
.sp
\fBenabled\fP
.RS 4
Print the currently enabled Solution.
.RE
.sp
\fBapplied\fP
.RS 4
Print the currently applied Solution.
.br
If one or more Notes of the Solution are \fBreverted\fP, which is
indicated by a \*(Aq\-\*(Aq in the output of \fBsaptune note list\fP, the string
\fB(partial)\fP is added to the Solution name.
.RE
\" _strm_3.2.0_start
.sp
\fBsimulate \- ATTENTION: deprecated\fP
.RS 4
Show all Notes that are associated with the specified Solution, and
all changes that will be applied once the Solution is activated.
.RE
\" _strm_3.2.0_end
.sp
\fBverify\fP
.RS 4
If a Solution name is specified, \fBsaptune\fP verifies the running system
against all Notes of that Solution regardless of their state.
.RE
.sp
\fBedit\fP
.RS 4
This allows to edit the Note list of the customer or vendor specific
Solution definitions in \fI/etc/saptune/extra\fP. An editor will be
launched to allow changing the Note definitions. The editor is defined
by the \fBEDITOR\fP environment variable. If not set editor defaults to
\fI/usr/bin/vim\fP.
.br
\~
.br
You can change, add or delete NoteIDs in the list of Notes defining
the Solution.
.br
\~
.br
If the Solution is currently applied and/or an override file exists,
saptune will remind you to take care of this situation.
.RE
.sp
\fBcustomise\fP
.RS 4
This allows to customize the Note list of the \fBsaptune\fP Solution
definitions. The Solution definition file will be copied from
\fI/usr/share/saptune/sols\fP or \fI/etc/saptune/extra\fP to the override
location at \fI/etc/saptune/override\fP, if the file does not exist
already. After that an editor will be launched to allow changing the
Solution definitions. The editor is defined by the \fBEDITOR\fP
environment variable. If not set editor defaults to \fI/usr/bin/vim\fP.
.br
\~
.br
You can change, add or delete NoteIDs in the list of Notes defining
the Solution.
.br
\~
.br
The values from the override files will take precedence over the
values from \fI/usr/share/saptune/sols\fP or \fI/etc/saptune/extra\fP. In such
case you will not lose your customized Solutions between \fBsaptune\fP or
vendor updates.
.br
The \fBsaptune\fP option \*(Aqlist\*(Aq will mark the existence of an override file.
.br
\~
.br
ATTENTION: Creating or changing an override file just changes the
configuration \fIinside\fP this Solution definition file, but does not
change the \fIrunning\fP configuration of the system.
.br
That means: When creating or changing an override file for an \fBalready
applied\fP Solution definition, please do a \fBsaptune solution revert
SOLUTIONNAME\fP and then apply this Solution again, to get the
changes take effect.
.RE
.sp
\fBcreate\fP
.RS 4
This allows to create own Solution definition files in
\fI/etc/saptune/extra\fP. The Solution definition file will be created
from a template file into the location \fI/etc/saptune/extra\fP, if the
file does not exist already. After that an editor will be launched to
allow changing the Solution definition. The editor is defined by the
\fBEDITOR\fP environment variable. If not set editor defaults to
/usr/bin/vim. You need to choose an unique Solution name for this
operation. Use \fBsaptune solution list\fP to find the already used
Solution names.
.RE
.sp
\fBrevert\fP
.RS 4
Revert optimization settings recommended by the Solution, and these
settings will no longer be activated automatically upon system boot.
.RE
.sp
\fBchange\fP
.RS 4
Switch to a new Solution even that another Solution was already
applied.
.br
\~
.br
ATTENTION:
.br
because of the revert of the old Solution during the execution of the
action \*(Aqchange\*(Aq the system will be not sufficient tuned for SAP
workloads for a short period of time until the new Solution is applied
successfully. This may harm a running SAP system. So use this action
carefully.
.br
And please keep in mind: Because of the \*(Aqrevert\*(Aq and \*(Aqapply\*(Aq the order
of Notes and therefore the active tuning may change, especially if
additional Notes were applied beside the old applied Solution.
.RE
.sp
\fBshow\fP
.RS 4
Print content of Solution definition file to stdout.
.RE
.sp
\fBdelete\fP
.RS 4
This allows to delete a customer or vendor specific Solution
definition file including the corresponding override file if
available. A confirmation is needed to finish the action.
.br
\~
.br
ATTENTION:
.br
Solution definition files shipped by the \fBsaptune\fP package \- so called
\fIinternal\fP \fBsaptune\fP Solution definition files \- \fBmust not\fP be deleted.
There will be an appropriate error message.
.br
If a corresponding override file is available, there will be the
possibility to delete this file instead.
.br
\~
.br
ATTENTION:
.br
If the Solution is already applied, the command will be terminated
with the information, that the Solution first needs to be reverted
before it can be deleted.
.RE
.sp
\fBrename\fP
.RS 4
This allows to rename a customer or vendor specific Solution
definition file to a new name. If a corresponding override file is
available, this file will be renamed too. A confirmation is needed to
finish the action.
.br
If the \fBnew\fP Solution definition name already exists the command will
be terminated with a respective message.
.br
\~
.br
ATTENTION:
.br
Solution definition files shipped by the \fBsaptune\fP package \- so called
\fIinternal\fP \fBsaptune\fP Solution definition files \- and their corresponding
override files, if available, \fBmust not\fP be renamed. There will be an
appropriate error message.
.br
\~
.br
ATTENTION:
.br
If the Solution is already applied, the command will be terminated
with the information, that the Solution first needs to be reverted
before it can be renamed.
.RE
.SH "STAGING ACTIONS"
.sp
Staging is implemented to enable customers to control and release
changes shipped by package updates to their working environment.
.br
The basic idea is, that Note definition files shipped by \fBsaptune\fP in
updates are not used by \fBsaptune\fP for system tuning immediately. An
administrator has to explicitly release the updates before being used by
\fBsaptune\fP. This allows customers to update \fBsaptune\fP, even with changed
Notes, without having changes in system behavior.
.sp
Staging is disabled by default, as not every customer needs the feature
and having it enabled by default would break the previous behavior.
.sp
Staging can be enable by \*(Aq\fBsaptune staging enable\fP\*(Aq (see description
below).
.sp
So now we will have 3 areas, where Note definition files and Solution
definitions shipped by \fBsaptune\fP can reside:
.br
This is only related to Note definition files and Solution definitions
shipped by \fBsaptune\fP. Custom definition files or override files are \fBNOT\fP
affected.
.sp
\fBPackage Area\fP
.br
Directory where the \fBsaptune\fP package stores and maintains shipped Note
definition files and Solution definitions. This directory gets changed
by RPM package operations solely.
.br
Currently this is \fI/usr/share/saptune/\fP.
.sp
\fBWorking Area\fP
.br
Directory which contains configuration objects \fBsaptune
note|solution|daemon|service\fP will use. Only Notes and Solutions from
here can be applied.
.br
Currently this is \fI/var/lib/saptune/working/\fP.
.sp
\fBStaging Area\fP
.br
Directory where configuration objects are stored, which are present in
the package area but differ from the objects in the working area.
.br
Currently this is \fI/var/lib/saptune/staging/\fP.
.br
At the moment only the Notes from the last update are kept in
\fI/var/lib/saptune/staging/latest\fP.
.sp
\fBstatus\fP
.RS 4
Displays the status of staging, basically the content of the variable
STAGING in the central \fBsaptune\fP configuration file.
.RE
.sp
\fBis\-enabled\fP
.RS 4
Returns the status of staging, basically the content of the variable
STAGING in the central \fBsaptune\fP configuration file, as exit code (0 ==
enabled, 1 == disabled). No output is generated as this is meant to be
used in scripts.
.RE
.sp
\fBenable|disable\fP
.RS 4
Enables or disables staging, by setting the variable STAGING in the
central \fBsaptune\fP configuration file. The result of the status change is
displayed.
.br
\~
.br
Altering the setting does not changes the content of the staging and
working directory.
.br
If a user disables staging an package updated might clean the staging
area and update the working area.
.RE
.sp
\fBlist\fP
.RS 4
Lists all Notes and the Solution definition which can be released from
the staging area including name, description, version and release
date.
.br
The Solution definition is shown as a whole object. It is only
possible to release the entire definition, but not single Solutions.
.br
Lastly a hint is printed to remind the user that he has to release
staged objects before he can use them and that it is possible to view
the changes.
.RE
.sp
\fBdiff [ ( NOTEID | SOLUTIONNAME.sol )... | all ]\fP
.RS 4
Shows the differences between the Note (or all Notes) or the Solution
definition in the staging area and the working area.
.br
For each Note in the staging area the output contains the values of
all parameter which differ. This includes new or removed parameters as
well as changes in the reminder section.
.br
For the Solution, all changed Solutions are displayed with their
differences.
.br
Lastly a hint is printed to remind the user that he has to release
staged objects before he can use them.
.RE
.sp
\fBanalysis [ ( NOTEID | SOLUTIONNAME.sol )... | all ]\fP
.RS 4
Does an analysis of the requested Notes, the Solution definitions or
everything in the staging area to warn the user about possible issues
or additional steps to perform.
.br
Lastly a hint is printed to remind the user that he has to release
staged objects before he can use them as well that he should check out
the differences.
.RE
.sp
\fBrelease [ ( NOTEID | SOLUTIONNAME.sol )... | all ]\fP
.RS 4
Releases the requested Notes, the Solution definitions or everything
in the stages area.
.br
This means the Notes or the Solution definitions get moved from the
staging area to the working area. In case of a deleted Note/Solution,
it will be removed from the working area.
.br
First the command will show an analysis of the objects going to be
released to make the user aware of further needed actions or potential
problems (for details see \fBsaptune\fP staging dependencies).
.br
\~
.br
Because the release is irreversible, the user has to confirm the
action.
.RE
.SH "CONFIGURE ACTIONS"
.sp
Replaces the direct editing of the \fBsaptune\fP configuration file
\fI/etc/sysconfig/saptune\fP in SLES for SAP 15, which will be replaced by an
intern configuration file in SLE 16 and not be present in future
versions.
.br
Not all of the former variables will be available as configure option,
only those who should be changeable by the user.
.br
.sp
\fBCOLOR_SCHEME SCHEME\fP
.RS 4
Default color scheme. See \fBsaptune verify\fP in this man page for
available color schemes.
.br
To reset the \fBcolor scheme\fP use an empty value as SCHEME like
\fBsaptune configure COLOR_SCHEME\fP
.RE
.sp
\fBSKIP_SYSCTL_FILES FILE[,FILE...]\fP
.RS 4
Comma\-separated list of \fBsysctl\fP config files or directories which
should be excluded when checking if parameters handled by \fBsaptune\fP are
handled by \fBsysctl\fP as well. Input is checked, if the given files are in
a location which is searched by \fBsysctl\fP command. If not, the files will
be skipped.
.RE
.sp
\fBIGNORE_RELOAD yes|no\fP
.RS 4
Controls behavior of \fBsystemctl\fP reload \fBsaptune.service\fP and the
systemctl try\-restart \fBsaptune.service\fP during package installation.
.RE
.sp
\fBDEBUG on|off\fP
.RS 4
Turns on or off the DebugLog output.
.br
\~
.br
ATTENTION: Active DebugLog will clutter the output of \fBsaptune\fP commands
and can render the output hard to read. Additional it is flooding the
log file with debug information. So use this setting with caution.
Only turn it on, if instructed by SUSE support to do so.
.RE
.sp
\fBTrentoASDP <period>|off\fP
.RS 4
Sets the discovery period (\fBsaptune\-discovery\-period\fP) of the Trento
Agent in \fI/etc/trento/agent.yaml\fP for collecting \fBsaptune\fP information
from the system.
.br
The discovery period is the time in seconds between 2 \fBsaptune\fP calls
initiated by the Trento Agent \- the tick interval for the \fBsaptune\fP
discovery loop. Adapting this value can prevent \fBsaptune\fP currently in
use, try later\*(Aq errors (exit code \fB11\fP) from \fBsaptune\fP.
.br
Trento will start with a default of 900s
.br
Possible values are "300", "600", "900", "1800" and "3600" seconds or
"off" to disable the check during start of \fBsaptune\fP.
\~
.br
If the variable is set \fBsaptune\fP will check during start if the value of
the variable found in the \fBsaptune\fP configuration file is valid and
still set in \fI/etc/trento/agent.yaml\fP. If not a warning will pop up.
.br
Setting \fBTrentoASDP\fP to "off" will disable the check during start of
\fBsaptune\fP. The setting of \fBsaptune\-discovery\-period\fP in the Trento Agent
config is not affected by this setting.
.RE
.sp
\fBCSP_TIMEOUT <int>\fP
.RS 4
Integer value representing the connection timeout for the cloud
Metadata Server query used for the cloud instance detection. Default
is 5 seconds.
.RE
.sp
\fBCSP_RETRIES <int>\fP
.RS 4
Number of retries for the cloud Metadata Server query used for the
cloud instance detection. Default is 3 retries.
.RE
.sp
\fBCLOUD_DETECTION always|once|first\fP
.RS 4
Configures the frequency of cloud and instance detection. When the
variable is set, a cloud and instance detection is triggered
immediately.
.br
If the instance type and cloud vendor have been set manually, the
variable gets ignored!
.br
The default is \fBfirst\fP.
.br
Possible values are:
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.  sp -1
.  IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
\fBalways\fP
.br
Detection is done at each \fBsaptune\fP invocation.
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.  sp -1
.  IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
\fBonce\fP
.br
Detection is done only once when the configuration is empty.
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.  sp -1
.  IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
\fBfirst\fP
.br
Detection is done the first time \fBsaptune\fP is called on a running system
(\fBsystemctl is\-system\-running\fP reports the system as \fBrunning\fP or
\fBdegraded\fP) after every boot.
.RE
.RE
.sp
\fBreset\fP
.RS 4
Reverts the tuning and reset the content of the \fBsaptune\fP configuration
file to the installation default. Asks for confirmation.
.RE
.sp
\fBshow\fP
.RS 4
Shows the content of the \fBsaptune\fP configuration file
.RE
.SH "CLOUD ACTIONS"
.sp
\fBget instance_type\fP
.RS 4
Returns the stored instance type and if the information was set
manually or has been determined by detection.
.RE
.sp
\fBclear instance_type\fP
.RS 4
Clears the stored instance type as well as the manual set flag.
.RE
.sp
\fBdetect instance_type\fP
.RS 4
Runs the instance detection, stores the instance type and flags the
setting as detected by \fBsaptune\fP. The result of the detection will be
printed to stdout too.
.RE
.sp
\fBset instance_type CLOUD_VENDOR%INSTANCE_TYPE\fP
.RS 4
Sets the cloud vendor and the instance type and flags the setting as
manually set. An empty value will not be accepted.
.br
There is no check, if the system is currently running on the given
cloud vendor and instance type. This allows to change the cloud
configuration for further use in a different instance type than
currently used.
.RE
.SH "VERIFY ACTIONS"
.sp
\fBverify applied\fP
.RS 4
Verifies all system parameters against all applied Notes.
.br
Same as a \fBsaptune note verify\fP.
.RE
.SH "REFRESH ACTIONS"
.sp
\fBrefresh applied *ATTENTION:\fP experimental*
.RS 4
Refreshes all applied Notes.
.br
Same as a \fBsaptune note refresh\fP.
.RE
.SH "REVERT ACTIONS"
.sp
\fBrevert all\fP
.RS 4
Revert all optimization settings recommended by the SAP Solution
and/or the Notes, and these settings will no longer be activated
automatically upon system boot.
.RE
.SH "CHECK ACTIONS"
.sp
\fBcheck\fP
.RS 4
Will simply call the external script \fI/usr/sbin/saptune_check\fP.
.RE
.SH "STATUS ACTIONS"
.sp
\fBstatus\fP
.RS 4
Will display the currently \fBsaptune\fP status. This will be short for
\*(Aqsaptune service status\*(Aq.
.RE
.SH "VERSION ACTIONS"
.sp
\fBversion\fP
.RS 4
Will display the currently active \fBsaptune\fP version.
.RE
.SH "HELP ACTIONS"
.sp
\fBhelp\fP
.RS 4
Will display the syntax of \fBsaptune\fP.
.RE
.SH "VENDOR SUPPORT"
.sp
To support vendor or customer specific tuning values, saptune supports
\*(Aqdrop\-in\*(Aq files residing in \fI/etc/saptune/extra\fP. All files found in
\fI/etc/saptune/extra\fP are listed when running \*(Aq\fBsaptune note list\fP\*(Aq. All
\fBNote options\fP are available for these files.
.sp
We simplified the file name syntax for these vendor files.
.br
Related to this we add \*(Aqheader\*(Aq support (see description of section
\fB[version]\fP in \fBsaptune\-note\fP(5)) for the vendor files as already available
for the Note definition files in \fI/usr/share/saptune/notes\fP to get a
proper description during \fBsaptune\fP option \*(Aqlist\*(Aq
.br
\" _strm_3.2.0_start
.sp
The old file names are still valid, but \fBdeprecated\fP. The support will
be dropped in the near future. That means, files without a valid header
information (see description of section \fB[version]\fP in \fBsaptune\-note\fP(5))
will be skipped in the future.
\" _strm_3.2.0_end
.sp
\fBSyntax of the file names:\fP
.br
\fINOTEID.conf\fP
.br
e.g. \fIV4711.conf\fP
\" _strm_3.2.0_start
.sp
old syntax of the file names: \fINOTEID\-DESCRIPTION\fP
.br
e.g. \fIVendor\-Recommended_OS_Settings\fP or \fISAP4711\-very_aromatic_tunings\fP
\" _strm_3.2.0_end
.sp
\fBSyntax of the file:\fP
.br
The content of the \*(Aqdrop\-in\*(Aq file should be written
in a INI file style with sections headed by \*(Aq[section_name]\*(Aq keywords.
See \fBsaptune\-note\fP(5) to find the supported sections and their available
options.
.sp
ATTENTION: If renaming or removing an active (aka \*(Aqalready applied\*(Aq)
Note definition file from the file system the \fBold\fP name of this Note
still remains in the configuration of saptune. This may lead to
unexpected messages.
.br
So please always revert the Note \fBbefore\fP renaming or removing it from
the file system.
.br
Even if editing an active vendor or customer specific Note definition
file on the file system level, please do a revert of that Note and then
apply the Note again, to get the changes take effect.
.sp
\fBCustomer specific Solution definitions\fP
.br
In addition to the vendor or customer specific Note file definitions
described previously \fBsaptune\fP now supports vendor or customer specific
\fBSolution definitions\fP by using \*(Aqdrop\-in\*(Aq files in \fI/etc/saptune/extra\fP.
All Solutions found in \fI/etc/saptune/extra\fP are listed when running
\*(Aq\fBsaptune solution list\fP\*(Aq. All \fBSolution options\fP are available for
these Solutions.
.sp
\fBSyntax of the Solution definition file names:\fP
\fISOLUTIONNAME.sol\fP
.br
e.g. \fIV4711.sol\fP
.SH "ATTENTION"
.sp
Trento support: If you plan to use Trento and its capability of checking
the correctness of the configuration of your SAP environment please
adapt the following Solution name schema for your custom Solutions:
.br
\fI<saptune_shipped_Solutionname>{underscore}<your_preferred_badge>.sol\fP
.br
e.g. \fINETWEAVER_MyOwnSolution.sol\fP
.sp
Syntax of the file: The content of the custom specific Solution files
should be written in a INI file style with sections headed by
\fB[section_name]\fP keywords.
.br
At the moment \fBsaptune\fP supports two architectures \- \fIArchX86\fP for the x86
platform and \fIArchPPC64LE\fP for 64\-bit PowerPC little endian platform for
the Solution definitions.
.br
So possible sections for Solution definitions are [version] (see
description of section [version] in \fBsaptune\-note\fP(5)) for a brief
description of the Solutions, and \fB[ArchX86]\fP and \fB[ArchPPC64LE]\fP for the
Solution definitions.
.br
The Solution itself is described as a list of Note definition files
separated by blanks. The Solution \fBname\fP is defined by the filename
without the \fI.sol\fP suffix. A Solution is only valid and listed by
\fBsaptune solution list\fP, if all listed Note definition files can be
found in the working area or in \fI/etc/saptune/extra\fP.
.sp
e.g. filename is \fINEWSOL1.sol\fP with content
.sp
[version]
.br
VERSION=1
.br
DATE=15.12.2020
.br
DESCRIPTION=My custom specific Solution definitions
.br
REFERENCES=
.br
[ArchX86]
.br
1980196 CUSTOMNOTE1 CUSTOMNOTE2
.br
[ArchPPC64LE]
.br
1980196 CUSTOMNOTE1 CUSTOMNOTE2
.SH "EXIT CODES"
.sp
All saptune commands share the following exit codes:
.RS 3
.ll -.6i
.sp
0
.RS 4
Success: The requested command completed successfully, or the command was a pure informational query
.RE
.sp
1
.RS 4
General Failure / Syntax Error / Permission Denied: Unrecognized command\-line arguments, invalid flags,
missing parameters, execution failure, or invoked without root privileges
.RE
.sp
11
.RS 4
Lock Collision: Another instance of saptune is currently running and holds /run/.saptune.lock
.RE
.sp
12
.RS 4
Lock Creation Error: Problems creating or setting the lock file \fI/run/.saptune.lock\fP
.RE
.sp
99
.RS 4
Lock File Corruption: Error reading or parsing the PID from \fI/run/.saptune.lock\fP
.RE
.sp
128
.RS 4
Central Configuration Error: The saptune configuration file cannot be read, mandatory keys are missing, STAGING has an invalid value, SAPTUNE_VERSION does not match, or cloud instance
detection/storage failed
.RE
.sp
130
.RS 4
JSON Output Serialization Failure: An error occurred while serializing the machine\-readable JSON structure
.RE
.br
.RE
.ll
.sp
The following commands have individual exit codes additionally or
alternatively:
.sp
\fBsaptune status\fP, \fBsaptune service status\fP, \fBsaptune daemon status\fP
.RS 4
.sp
0
.RS 4
\fBsaptune.service\fP is active, the system is tuned, and all applied settings are compliant.
.RE
.sp
1
.RS 4
\fBsaptune.service\fP is not active / stopped.
.RE
.sp
2
.RS 4
not used
.RE
.sp
3
.RS 4
\fBsaptune.service\fP has been started, but tuning (Note or Solution) is not configured.
.RE
.sp
4
.RS 4
\fBsaptune.service\fP has been started, system is tuned, but tuning is not compliant — one or more parameters deviated from recommendations.
.RE
.RE
.sp
\fBsaptune note|solution verify\fP, \fBsaptune verify applied\fP
.RS 4
.sp
0
.RS 4
All evaluated parameters conform to recommendations (or no Notes/Solutions are enabled/applied to check).
.RE
.sp
1
.RS 4
One or more parameters deviated from recommendations (non\-compliant) OR an inspection error occurred / specified Note or Solution does not exist.
.RE
.RE
.sp
\fBsaptune staging analysis\fP
.RS 4
.sp
0
.RS 4
All evaluated objects are releaseable with no administrative action required (e.g. new or un\-enabled definitions).
.RE
.sp
1
.RS 4
Advisory action required (e.g. Note or Solution is currently applied and must be re\-applied or refreshed after release, or benefits from an update).
.RE
.sp
2
.RS 4
Breaking release: Releasing the object would break \fBsaptune\fP functionality (e.g. a deleted Note is part of an active enabled Solution, or a required note is missing).
.RE
.RE
.sp
\fBsaptune staging release\fP
.RS 4
.sp
0
.RS 4
Objects successfully released to the working area; OR \fB\-\-dry\-run\fP completed without changes; OR release cancelled by user interaction; OR no staging files available.
.RE
.sp
1
.RS 4
Specified object not found in the staging area; OR an error occurred copying/moving files.
.RE
.sp
2
.RS 4
Release blocked because releasing the object would break \fBsaptune\fP functionality.
.RE
.RE
.sp
\fBsaptune staging is\-enabled\fP
.RS 4
.sp
0
.RS 4
Staging is enabled.
.RE
.sp
1
.RS 4
Staging is disabled.
.RE
.RE
.sp
\fBsaptune check\fP
.RS 4
.sp
0
.RS 4
All checks passed (or only advisory warnings found).
.RE
.sp
1
.RS 4
One or more checks failed; \fBsaptune\fP will not work properly.
.RE
.sp
2
.RS 4
Operating system is not SLES, or \fBsaptune\fP package is not installed.
.RE
.sp
3
.RS 4
Invalid/wrong command\-line parameter passed to \fBsaptune_check\fP.
.RE
.sp
5
.RS 4
Unexpected error regarding JSON output generation.
.RE
.RE
.SH "HOTFIX"
.sp
A HotFix is a new way to react fast to \fBcritical bugs\fP, that can harm
SAP installations.
.br
It is meant to bridge the time with a \fBtemporary mitigation\fP until the
\fBpermanent fix\fP is available. As soon as the fix is released, the next
released \fBsaptune\fP version will contain an \fBupdate\fP to the HotFix to be
applied only when necessary. \fBOne year\fP after the fix is available, we
will \fBremove\fP the HotFix from \fBsaptune\fP.
.sp
We hope to provide an comfortable way to address such issues.
.sp
The HotFix will be added as individual Note with the name
.br
\fBHotFixTID_NUMBER\fP and can be part of a Solution.
.br
A HotFix release will contain only configuration changes and no new
features.
.sp
To activate the HotFix you can simply apply the Note by
.sp
\fBsaptune note apply HotFix<TID_NUMBER>\fP
.br
which will not interrupt your current tuning but add the needed
parameter settings. The change will take effect immediately and is
reboot persistent.
.sp
Or you restart the \fBsaptune.service\fP, which will lead to a short gap in
your tuning because of the \*(Aqrevert\*(Aq/\*(Aqapply\*(Aq workflow of the service
restart. Your SAP workload should be stopped for this operation.
.sp
Or you reboot your server.
.sp
As soon as the permanent fix is available and installed on the system,
the Hotfix can be reverted with
.br
\fBsaptune note revert HotFix<TID_NUMBER>\fP
.sp
The next released \fBsaptune\fP version will contain an update of the HotFix
with a condition (\fBtag\fP) to apply this HotFix only under the condition
the permanent fix is not installed on the systems.
.br
The HotFix will be removed from \fBsaptune\fP one year after the permanent fix
is available, giving customers enough time to update their systems.
.SH "CHANGES"
.sp
\fBchangelog\fP
.RS 4
The changelog file of the \fBsaptune\fP rpm package contains detailed
information, what was changed between the various package versions.
The command
.br
\~
.br
\fBrpm \-q \-\-changes saptune | more\fP
.br
\~
.br
will show the content of the file
.RE
.sp
\fBversion 3\fP
.RS 4
With the update to \fBsaptune\fP version 3 \fBsaptune\fP does no longer use
\fBtuned\fP(8) to restart after a system reboot. It is using its own systemd
service named \fBsaptune.service\fP.
.br
So we now \fBdeprecated\fP all "DAEMON ACTIONS" like \fBsaptune daemon start\fP.
These commands will still work as they are internally linked
to the related "SERVICE ACTIONS" like \fBsaptune service takeover\fP.
Please refer to the related section descriptions at the top of this
man page.
.RE
.sp
\fBversion 3.1\fP
.RS 4
With the update to \fBsaptune\fP version 3.1 we \fBdeprecated\fP the actions
\fBsaptune note simulate\fP and \fBsaptune solution simulate\fP.
.RE
.sp
\fBversion 3.2\fP
.RS 4
With the update to \fBsaptune\fP version 3.2 on SLES for SAP 12 and 15 we
\fBdeprecated\fP the manual editing of the file \fI/etc/sysconfig/saptune\fP
as the main configuration file will be (re)moved in future versions.
.br
Additional the Solution \fBSAP\-ASE.sol\fP and the related Notes \fB1680803\fP
and \fB1805750\fP are \fBdeprecated\fP on SLES for SAP 12 and 15 and removed
in SLE 16.
.br
SAP Note \fB1771258\fP not relevant/valid anymore on SLE 16, we
\fBdeprecated\fP the Note and the support for setting PAM limits in SLES
for SAP 12 and 15 and removed both in SLE 16
.br
The already deprecated actions \*(Aqdaemon\*(Aq, \*(Aqnote simulate\*(Aq and \*(Aqsolution
simulate\*(Aq are removed in SLE16
.RE
.sp
\fBversion 3.2.3\fP
.RS 4
SAP Note \fB941735\fP not relevant/valid anymore, we \fBdeprecated\fP the Note
with the update to \fBsaptune\fP version 3.2.3 on SLES for SAP 12, 15 and 16
and on plain SLES 16.
.RE
.SH "FILES"
.sp
\fI/usr/share/saptune/schemas/1.0\fP
.RS 3
.ll -.6i
.sp
schemata defining the json output format available since \fBsaptune\fP version
3.1
.br
.RE
.ll
.sp
\fI/usr/share/saptune/schemas/1.1\fP
.RS 3
.ll -.6i
.sp
schemata defining the json output format available since \fBsaptune\fP version
3.2
.br
.RE
.ll
.sp
\fI/usr/share/saptune/notes\fP
.RS 3
.ll -.6i
.sp
part of the \fBPackage Area\fP
.br
the \fBsaptune\fP SAP Note definitions, which are shipped by the \fBsaptune\fP RPM
package
.br
Please do not change the files located here. You will lose all your
changes during a \fBsaptune\fP package update.
.br
.RE
.ll
.sp
\fI/usr/share/saptune/sols\fP
.RS 3
.ll -.6i
.sp
part of the \fBPackage Area\fP
.br
the saptune Solution definitions, which are shipped by the \fBsaptune\fP RPM
package
.sp
Please do not change as maintenance updates of package \fBsaptune\fP will
overwrite these files without preserving any custom changes.
.br
.RE
.ll
.sp
\fI/var/lib/saptune/working/notes\fP
.RS 3
.ll -.6i
.sp
part of the \fBWorking Area\fP
.br
the saptune SAP Note definitions, which can be listed by \fBsaptune note
list\fP
.sp
The files are named with the number of their corresponding SAP Note
(==NoteID).
.br
A description of the syntax and the available tuning options can be
found in \fBsaptune\-note\fP(5)
.br
Please do not change the files located here. You will lose all your
changes during a \fBsaptune staging release\fP command. Use override files
to change parameter values if needed.
.br
.RE
.ll
.sp
\fI/var/lib/saptune/working/sols\fP
.RS 3
.ll -.6i
.sp
part of the \fBWorking Area\fP
.br
the \fBsaptune\fP Solution definitions, which can be listed by \fBsaptune
solution list\fP
.br
At the moment saptune supports two architectures \- \fIArchX86\fP for the x86
platform and \fIArchPPC64LE\fP for 64\-bit PowerPC little endian platform \-
with different Solution definitions.
.sp
Please do not change the files located here as the command \fBsaptune
staging release\fP may overwrite these files without preserving any
custom changes. Use override files to change the Note list of the
Solutions.
.br
.RE
.ll
.sp
\fI/var/lib/saptune/staging/latest\fP
.RS 3
.ll -.6i
.sp
part of the \fBStaging Area\fP
.br
the \fBsaptune\fP SAP Note or Solution definitions, which are present in the
Package Area but differ from the files in the Working Area.
.br
.RE
.ll
.sp
\fI/var/lib/saptune/config/cloud.json\fP
.RS 3
.ll -.6i
.sp
the file where \fBsaptune\fP stores the detected cloud instance.
.br
Please use \fBsaptune cloud\fP command instead of editing the file
directly.
.br
.RE
.ll
.sp
\fI/etc/sysconfig/saptune\fP
.RS 3
.ll -.6i
.sp
the central \fBsaptune\fP configuration file in SLES for SAP \fB12 and 15\fP
containing the information about the currently enabled Notes and
Solutions, the order in which these Notes are applied and the version of
\fBsaptune\fP currently used.
.br
ATTENTION: the manual editing of this file will be \fBdeprecated\fP on SLES
for SAP 12 and 15 as the main configuration file will be (re)moved in
SLE 16.
.br
Please use the \fBsaptune configure\fP command instead of editing the file
directly.
.br
.RE
.ll
.sp
\fI/var/lib/saptune/config/saptune\fP
.RS 3
.ll -.6i
.sp
the central \fBsaptune\fP configuration file in SLES and SLES for SAP \fB16\fP
containing the information about the currently enabled Notes and
Solutions, the order in which these Notes are applied and the version of
\fBsaptune\fP currently used.
.br
ATTENTION: Do not edit the file directly! Use the \fBsaptune configure\fP
command instead.
.br
.RE
.ll
.sp
\fI/etc/saptune/extra\fP
.RS 3
.ll -.6i
.sp
vendor or customer specific tuning or Solution definitions.
.br
Please see \fBVENDOR SUPPORT\fP above for more information.
.sp
You can use \fBsaptune note create NOTEID\fP or \fBsaptune solution create
SOLUTIONNAME\fP to create a new vendor or customer specific definition or
\fBsaptune note edit NOTEID\fP or \fBsaptune solution edit SolutionName\fP
to modify an already existing vendor or customer specific definition.
.br
.RE
.ll
.sp
\fI/etc/saptune/override\fP
.RS 3
.ll -.6i
.sp
the \fBsaptune\fP Note and Solution definition override location.
.sp
If you need to customize the Note or Solution definitions found in
\fI/usr/share/saptune/notes\fP or \fI/usr/share/saptune/sols\fP or in
\fI/etc/saptune/extra\fP, you can copy them to \fI/etc/saptune/override\fP and
modify them as you need. Please stay with the original name of the Note
or Solution definition (the Note ID or Solution name) and do \fBNOT\fP rename
it.
.sp
If the shipped Note or Solution does \fBNOT\fP contain an untagged section
an override will not work.
.sp
Or use \fBsaptune note customize NOTEID\fP or \fBsaptune solution customize
SOLUTIONNAME\fP to do the job for you.
.br
.RE
.ll
.sp
\fI/run/saptune/saved_state/\fP \fI/run/saptune/parameter/\fP
.RS 3
.ll -.6i
.sp
\fBsaptune\fP was designed to preserve the state of the system before starting
the SAP specific tuning, so that it will be possible to restore this
previous state of the system, if the SAP specific tuning is no longer
needed or should be changed.
.sp
This system state is saved during the \*(Aqapply\*(Aq operation of \fBsaptune\fP in
the \fBsaptune\fP internal used files in \fI/run/saptune/saved_state\fP and
\fI/run/saptune/parameter\fP. The content of these files highly depends on the
previous state of the system.
.br
If the values are applied by \fBsaptune\fP, no further monitoring of the
system parameters are done, so changes of \fBsaptune\fP relevant parameters
will not be observed. If a SAP Note or a SAP Solution should be
reverted, then first the values read from the \fI/run/saptune/saved_state\fP
and \fI/run/saptune/parameter\fP files will be applied to the system to
restore the previous system state and then the corresponding save_state
file will be removed.
.sp
Please do not change or remove files in this directory. The knowledge
about the previous system state gets lost and the revert functionality
of \fBsaptune\fP will be destructed. So you will lose the capability to revert
back the tunings \fBsaptune\fP has done.
.br
.RE
.ll
.SH "NOTE"
.sp
Using \fBsaptune\fP within a pipe, the color information will be removed from
the output.
.br
It is possible to change this behavior by using the command line option
\fB\-\-force\-color\fP
.SH "NOTE"
.sp
When the values from the \fBsaptune\fP Note definitions are applied to the
system, no further monitoring of the system parameters are done. So
changes of \fBsaptune\fP relevant parameters by using the \*(Aqsysctl\*(Aq command or
by editing configuration files will not be observed. If the values set
by \fBsaptune\fP should be reverted, these unrecognized changed settings will
be overwritten by the previous saved system settings from \fBsaptune\fP.
.SH "NOTE"
.sp
To prevent unintended reload/restart of \fBsaptune\fP during package
installation/update of \fBsaptune\fP or normal work, which will result in a
short time period, where the system is not tuned for SAP workloads, it\(cqs
possible to set the parameter \fBIGNORE_RELOAD\fP in the central \fBsaptune\fP
configuration file \fI/etc/sysconfig/saptune\fP.
.br
\fBIGNORE_RELOAD\fP is used to control the \*(Aq\fBsystemctl reload
saptune.service\fP\*(Aq behavior.
.br
Default is \fBIGNORE_RELOAD="no"\fP, which means that the \*(Aqreload\*(Aq is
working as expected.
.br
If set to \fByes\fP a \fBsystemctl reload saptune.service\fP and a \fBsaptune
service restart\fP will do \fBnothing\fP. The reason will be logged.
.br
Additional this parameter setting will prevent \fBsystemctl restart
saptune.service\fP (which is a \*(AqExecStop\*(Aq followed by \*(AqExecStart\*(Aq) called
from macros used during the package installation/update of the \fBsaptune\fP
package from restarting the tuning.
.br
ATTENTION: Outside the \fBsaptune\fP package installation \fBsystemctl restart
saptune.service\fP can \fBnot\fP be blocked.
.SH "ATTENTION"
.sp
Higher or lower system values set by the system, the SAP installer or by
the administrator using sysctl command or sysctl configuration files
will be now \fBoverwritten\fP by \fBsaptune\fP, if they are part of the applied
Note definitions.
.sp
\fBsaptune\fP now sets the values read from the Note definition files
irrespective of already set higher system values. If you need other
tuning values as defined in the Note definition files, please use the
possibility to create \fBoverride\fP files, which contain the values you
need.
